export const glossary={
  particle:'One possible value of a hidden state. A particle is a point, not a small Gaussian distribution.',
  weight:'A nonnegative probability mass attached to a particle. Normalized weights sum to one.',
  posterior:'The probability distribution of an unknown state after using the observations seen so far.',
  resampling:'A step that replaces a weighted particle measure. Copying methods select ancestors; transform methods move particles.',
  covariance:'A matrix that describes spread along each coordinate and how pairs of coordinates vary together. This site uses population covariance.',
  eigenvalue:'For a covariance matrix, an eigenvalue is the variance along its associated eigenvector direction.',
  mode:'A local peak in probability density. Separate peaks can represent different plausible explanations of the same observation.',
  ESS:'Effective sample size: 1 divided by the sum of squared normalized weights. It measures weight concentration, not actual independence.',
  likelihood:'The probability density of the observed data as a function of model parameters. A density can exceed one.',
  score:'The gradient of the log likelihood with respect to model parameters.',
  gradient:'The vector of partial derivatives of a scalar objective. It describes local sensitivity to each parameter.',
  bias:'The expected estimate minus the reference value. The results use repeated runs to estimate this difference.',
  variance:'Expected squared variation around an estimator’s own mean. Low variance alone does not imply low error.',
  calibration:'Agreement between stated uncertainty and observed errors. For example, a nominal 95% region should contain the truth about 95% of the time under the evaluated setup.',
  whitening:'Centering a full-rank cloud and applying an inverse covariance square root so its population covariance becomes the identity matrix.',
  SPD:'Symmetric positive definite: a real symmetric matrix whose eigenvalues are strictly positive.',
  'closed form':'Here: an explicit matrix formula with no particle transport optimization loop. Numerical matrix factorizations still require computation.',
  'stop-gradient':'An automatic-differentiation operation that keeps a value in the forward pass but controls how its derivative is computed.',
  'optimal transport':'A way to couple two probability measures while minimizing a movement cost. Entropy regularization gives a smooth numerical transport problem.',
  'finite difference':'A numerical derivative estimated by perturbing a parameter. A central difference uses values at theta+h and theta-h.'
};
const note=(label,text)=>`<div class="callout"><strong>${label}</strong><p>${text}</p></div>`;
export const lessons=[
{id:'start',group:'START HERE',title:'Start here',subtitle:'From weighted particles to testable claims.',goal:'Understand the research goal and choose a path through the guide.',sections:[
 {id:'welcome',title:'Build intuition. Then test the mathematics.',html:`<p class="lead">We want a resampler with an explicit formula and useful first-order gradients. We also want it to represent uncertainty well. These are three separate goals. This guide helps you test each one.</p><div class="hero-demo" id="hero-demo"></div><p>You can start with basic probability. We introduce matrix concepts when you need them. Each lesson combines a short explanation, a live experiment, and the equations behind it.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button primary" href="#/particles">Start with particles <span>→</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="#/claims">See the research claims</a></div>`},
 {id:'path',title:'Your learning path',html:`<div class="path-list"><a href="#/particles"><span class="path-number">01</span><span><strong>See the probability distribution</strong><small>Particles, weights, and classical sampling.</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href="#/eigen"><span class="path-number">02</span><span><strong>Build the transformation</strong><small>Mean, covariance, matrix roots, and derivatives.</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href="#/metrics"><span class="path-number">03</span><span><strong>Turn claims into measurements</strong><small>Metrics, benchmark tasks, and measured evidence.</small></span><span>↗</span></a></div>`},
 {id:'status',title:'What we have built',html:`<p>The current method is an <strong>affine eigen moment-matching baseline</strong>. “Affine” means a linear transformation followed by a shift. Under full-rank conditions, it matches the weighted mean and population covariance.</p>${note('The research question','Can this inexpensive construction also give useful likelihood gradients and reliable uncertainty on the tasks we care about? Correct moments alone cannot answer that question.')}<div class="evidence-strip"><span><strong>6</strong> task families</span><span><strong>8</strong> source repositories</span><span><strong>CPU</strong> measured evidence</span></div><p>Live labs are small browser simulations. The results explorer uses exported measurements from the completed Python and Julia runs. Every plot identifies which kind of evidence it shows.</p>`}
],quiz:{prompt:'Which observation would establish all three research goals?',options:['The covariance error is zero.','The code runs backward without an error.','No single observation does; each goal needs its own test.'],answer:2,explanation:'Correct moments, a finite derivative, and useful filtering behavior are distinct properties.'},sources:['cuthbert','pfjax']},
{id:'particles',group:'FOUNDATIONS',title:'Particles and weights',subtitle:'Probability, represented by a small collection of points.',goal:'Read a weighted particle measure and calculate its mean and effective sample size.',sections:[
 {id:'idea',title:'One point is one possible state',html:raw`<p>Suppose you do not know where a target is. A <span data-term="particle">particle</span> is one possible position. Its <span data-term="weight">weight</span> tells you how much probability mass you assign to that position.</p><p>Weights must be nonnegative and sum to one. A large weight means “more plausible under this approximation.” It does not mean that point has a larger physical size.</p><div class="equation">$$p_N=\sum_{i=1}^N w_i\,\delta_{x_i},\qquad w_i\geq0,\quad\sum_iw_i=1.$$</div><p>Here, \(N\) is the number of particles, \(x_i\) is particle \(i\), and \(\delta_{x_i}\) is a unit point mass at \(x_i\). This is an <strong>empirical measure</strong>: a probability distribution supported on finitely many points.</p>`},
 {id:'try',title:'Move the probability mass',lab:'weights',html:`<p>Increase one particle’s weight. Watch the mean move and the effective sample size fall. You change the distribution even though every position stays fixed.</p>`},
 {id:'moments',title:'Summarize the cloud',html:raw`<p>The weighted mean is its center. The population covariance describes its spread:</p><div class="equation">$$\mu_w=\sum_iw_ix_i,\qquad C_w=\sum_iw_i(x_i-\mu_w)(x_i-\mu_w)^\top.$$</div><p>In one dimension, covariance is variance. We use population moments, so there is no Bessel correction.</p><p><span data-term="ESS">Effective sample size</span> (ESS) measures weight concentration:</p><div class="equation">$$\operatorname{ESS}=\frac{1}{\sum_iw_i^2},\qquad1\leq\operatorname{ESS}\leq N.$$</div><p>Equal weights give ESS \(=N\). All mass on one point gives ESS \(=1\). ESS does not detect duplicate positions: copying one position \(N\) times with equal weights still gives ESS \(=N\).</p>`}
],quiz:{prompt:'Four particles have equal weights. What is their ESS?',options:['1','4','It depends on their positions.'],answer:1,explanation:'ESS = 1 / (4 × 0.25²) = 4. Position diversity is a separate property.'},sources:['stone','douc']},
{id:'filter',group:'FOUNDATIONS',title:'Why resample?',subtitle:'Follow a hidden state through predict, weight, and resample.',goal:'Locate resampling in a particle filter and compare a toy filter with an exact reference.',sections:[
 {id:'cycle',title:'The filtering cycle',html:`<p>A particle filter estimates a hidden state from a sequence of noisy observations. Its <span data-term="posterior">posterior</span> changes as new data arrive.</p><div class="flow"><div><b>1</b><strong>Predict</strong><span>Move particles through the state model.</span></div><i>→</i><div><b>2</b><strong>Weight</strong><span>Score how well each explains the observation.</span></div><i>→</i><div><b>3</b><strong>Resample</strong><span>Replace a concentrated weighted cloud.</span></div></div><p>After many updates, a few particles can carry almost all the weight. Resampling reallocates the finite particle budget. Propagation then supplies new randomness. Copying particles alone does not create new distinct positions.</p>`},
 {id:'simulator',title:'Track a noisy signal',lab:'filter',html:raw`<p>This teaching model is \(x_t=0.8x_{t-1}+0.5\epsilon_t\), \(y_t=x_t+r\eta_t\), with \(x_0\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\) and independent standard normal noises. Because it is linear Gaussian, a Kalman filter supplies an exact posterior.</p><p>Change the particle count or observation noise. Scrub through time. The particle band shows mean ± 1.96 posterior standard deviations; it is a Gaussian approximation, not an exact empirical credible interval.</p>`},
 {id:'interpret',title:'What the simulation can show',html:raw`<p>We resample when ESS falls below \(N/2\). This is a common heuristic. It balances weight concentration against the extra randomness and particle duplication introduced by copying methods.</p><p>The log-likelihood estimate adds the log predictive density at each step. For a standard correctly weighted particle filter, usual unbiased-normalizer results need specific sampling assumptions. The deterministic affine transformation does not inherit those results automatically.</p><p>A single run illustrates behavior. To estimate bias, variance, or calibration, repeat the filter on fixed data and across independent data sequences. The real benchmark does that.</p>`}
],quiz:{prompt:'Does an equal-weight output prove that particle diversity improved?',options:['Yes; ESS is now N.','No; many particles can be exact copies.','Only if N is greater than 100.'],answer:1,explanation:'Weight concentration and diversity of positions are different. Equal weights can hide severe duplication.'},sources:['kalman','cuthbert']},
{id:'classical',group:'FOUNDATIONS',title:'Classical methods',subtitle:'Use a cumulative distribution to select particle ancestors.',goal:'Compare four copying methods and understand conditional unbiasedness.',sections:[
 {id:'cdf',title:'Copy positions in proportion to weight',html:raw`<p>Classical resampling produces ancestor indices \(A_j\). The new position is \(\widetilde x_j=x_{A_j}\), with output weight \(1/N\). Build the cumulative distribution function (CDF), draw points on \([0,1)\), and find the intervals containing them.</p><p><strong>Multinomial</strong> draws all points independently. <strong>Systematic</strong> uses one random offset and evenly spaced points. <strong>Stratified</strong> draws one point per equal-width interval. <strong>Residual</strong> first gives particle \(i\) exactly \(\lfloor Nw_i\rfloor\) copies, then samples the remainder.</p>`},
 {id:'selection',title:'Try the selection step',lab:'classical',html:`<p>The colors identify ancestors. Run one draw, or repeat 500 draws to estimate the added variation. Reorder the same particles to see how CDF order affects a systematic draw.</p>`},
 {id:'unbiased',title:'The useful guarantee',html:raw`<p>Let \(n_i\) be the number of copies of particle \(i\). Conditional on the input cloud, these classical methods satisfy:</p><div class="equation">$$\mathbb E[n_i\mid x,w]=Nw_i,$$$$\mathbb E\left[\frac1N\sum_jf(\widetilde x_j)\,\middle|\,x,w\right]=\sum_iw_if(x_i).$$</div><p>This is <strong>conditional unbiasedness</strong>. It holds for any integrable test function \(f\), not only for a mean or covariance. A single resampled cloud still has error.</p><p>Variance comparisons depend on the method and assumptions. Systematic resampling can be sensitive to particle order. Sorting, shuffling, or interleaving modes changes its behavior. The real mixture results make this effect visible.</p><div class="callout"><strong>A different contract</strong><p>The eigen method generates new positions. It has no discrete offspring counts and does not automatically satisfy the classical conditional-unbiasedness equation. We must test nonlinear expectations and shape error separately.</p></div>`}
],quiz:{prompt:'Does conditional unbiasedness mean every draw has the exact weighted mean?',options:['Yes.','No; the average over repeated draws has that mean.','It applies only to Gaussian inputs.'],answer:1,explanation:'The expectation is over resampling randomness. Individual draws can differ from the weighted input.'},sources:['douc','stone','book']},
...advancedLessons(references,glossary)
];
